.DS_Store
.build/
build/
*.xcodeproj/xcuserdata/
DerivedData/

# The crew is local: the agent briefs live on disk and are not part of the
# project's history, because they are how one person drives this repository,
# not something it ships. `agents` and `crew` are symbolic links into Core
# Crew for that same reason — `install.sh` creates both under "2. Project
# trees and their worktrees" (`link_dir "$t/.claude/agents" ...` and
# `link_dir "$t/.claude/crew" ...`), and `ls -la .claude` in a checkout it has
# run against shows the pair pointing into Core Crew.
#
# `/.claude/agents` and `/.claude/crew` carry no trailing slash because each
# names a symbolic link: a pattern ending in `/` matches a directory and not a
# symbolic link, so the moment a tracked copy becomes a link it would
# reappear in `git status`. `.build/`, `build/`, `*.xcodeproj/xcuserdata/`,
# `DerivedData/` above and `/.superpowers/` below carry the slash because each
# of those names an ordinary directory that install.sh never turns into a link.
/.claude/agents
/.claude/crew

# Only `docs/` stays out of the tree: plans and specs are a record of decisions
# being made, not of what the program is. ARCHITECTURE.md, CLAUDE.md,
# CHANGELOG.md and README.md describe the program itself and belong in the
# repository with it.
/docs

# Brainstorm sessions write HTML mockups here. They are working material for one
# design conversation — the decisions that survive them land in `docs/`, and the
# mockups themselves are worth keeping on disk and out of the history.
/.superpowers/
